data.content:
Here's the refactored test using TUnit's native exception assertion:

```csharp
using TUnit.Core;

public class OrderProcessorTests
{
    [Test]
    public async Task ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsInvalidOperation()
    {
        var processor = new OrderProcessor();

        await Assert.That(() => processor.ProcessOrder(new Order()))
            .ThrowsException<InvalidOperationException>()
            .WithMessage("Order must contain at least one item");
    }
}
```

This uses TUnit's fluent `ThrowsException<T>()` to assert the exception type and chains `.WithMessage(...)` to verify the message — all in one idiomatic expression, eliminating the try/catch boilerplate.
